Falsifies `system_tests::user::tests::a_user_program_cannot_read_a_kernel_address`.

Architecture: aarch64, x86_64

The defect is the kernel mapping its own constants EL0-readable: one flag, in the one place
the kernel image's sections are mapped at their linked addresses. `Flags::user_rodata()` is
`CAP_USER` alone, so the leaf encoder writes `AP_RO_EL0` where it wrote `AP_RO_EL1`, and
nothing else about the page changes: still not writable, still not executable at either
level. The kernel goes on reading its own `.rodata` because this kernel never sets
`PSTATE.PAN`, which `arch::aarch64::mmu::permit_kernel_access_to_user_pages` already records
as a no-op for exactly that reason.

The sentinel the test reaches for is a `static` in the kernel's `.rodata`, so this is the
page it is about.

**Nothing upstream catches it, and that was checked rather than assumed.** The obvious
candidate is `TCR_EL1.E0PD1`, which would fault an EL0 access to the TTBR1 range whatever the
access permission says and would make this patch a red for the wrong reason. This kernel does
not set it: `TCR_EL1` is written once with `T0SZ/TG0/SH0/ORGN0/IRGN0/EPD0/T1SZ/TG1/SH1/ORGN1/
IRGN1/EPD1/IPS` and nothing else. So the permission bit really is the only thing between EL0
and the kernel's constants here, which is what the test says.

Expected to fail: `assert!(wait_for(|| USER_FAULTS.load(Ordering::Relaxed) > before), "the
user program read a kernel address and was NOT stopped")`.

**The two sibling assertions below it never run**, and that is the honest half. The test goes
on to require a `Permission` fault rather than a `Translation` one, and to require the fault
address to be the sentinel's. Both are about a fault that no longer happens, so the test's
most interesting sentence, the one about permission-versus-translation, is not the one that
catches a kernel mapped user-readable; the fault counter is.

**This record is red on aarch64 and on x86_64, and cannot be on riscv64.** The aarch64 hunk
is the one above. The x86_64 hunk is the same one-flag defect at the same call site in
`arch/x86_64/mmu.rs`, which maps `.rodata` with `Flags::kernel_rodata()`; `user_rodata()` there
writes the `U/S` bit, and the kernel goes on reading its own constants because this kernel never
sets `CR4.SMAP`. Only `CR4.SMEP` is set, which stops a fetch from a user page and has no bearing
on a read, so nothing upstream of the page-table bit catches it. Replayed on 2026-10-03 (UTC) with
`cargo xtask test --arch x86_64 --test a_user_program_cannot_read_a_kernel_address`: red at
`system_tests/src/user/tests.rs:257` on the same assertion as aarch64, "the user program read a
kernel address and was NOT stopped".

The riscv64 leg is the reason the other two share a record rather than the claim being uniform.
`crates/paging`'s RISC-V encoder turns `CAP_USER` into the Sv39 `U` bit, which forbids S-mode
access unless `sstatus.SUM` is set. So the same defect there does not produce a kernel that lets
userspace read it; it produces a kernel that cannot read itself, and dies before any test runs.
riscv64's evidence for row 21 is the software walk in
`riscv_virtio_tests::the_page_tables_say_u_mode_cannot_read_the_kernels_memory`. See
notes/confinement-claims.md row 21.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/aarch64/mmu.rs b/kernel/src/arch/aarch64/mmu.rs
index 746341dd..87854474 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/aarch64/mmu.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/aarch64/mmu.rs
@@ -225,7 +225,7 @@ where
 
     // 2. The kernel image, section by section, at its LINKED virtual addresses. This is W^X.
     map_range(m, text_start(), text_end(), Flags::kernel_code())?;
-    map_range(m, rodata_start(), rodata_end(), Flags::kernel_rodata())?;
+    map_range(m, rodata_start(), rodata_end(), Flags::user_rodata())?;
     map_range(m, data_start(), bss_end(), Flags::kernel_data())?;
 
     // 3. THE GUARD PAGE IS NOT MAPPED. That is its entire job.
diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/mmu.rs b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/mmu.rs
index eb6a10193..f274c82be 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/mmu.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/mmu.rs
@@ -1348,7 +1348,7 @@ where
